<given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Minassian</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lopez</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Maron</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Lincoln</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sensorimotor gating deficits in adults with autism</article-title><source><italic>Biological Psychiatry</italic></source><year>2007</year><volume>61</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>482</fpage><lpage>486</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16460695</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B9"><label>5</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hawkins</surname><given-names>RD</given-names></name><name><surname>Cohen</surname><given-names>TE</given-names></name><name><surname>Kandel</surname><given-names>ER</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Dishabituation in <italic>Aplysia</italic> can involve either reversal of habituation or superimposed sensitization</article-title><source><italic>Learning &#x00026; Memory</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>13</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>397</fpage><lpage>403</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16705138</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B14"><label>6</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kupfermann</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Castellucci</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Pinsker</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Kandel</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Neuronal correlates of habituation and dishabituation of the gill-withdrawal reflex in <italic>Aplysia</italic></article-title><source><italic>Science</italic></source><year>1970</year><volume>167</volume><issue>3926</issue><fpage>1743</fpage><lpage>1745</lpage><pub-id pub-id-t